    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf": [
      {
        "@id": "http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E74_Group"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A state body with responsibility for heritage (a CIDOC-CRM E74 Group), e.g. the Guthi Sansthan (Guthi Corporation, a public corporation that administers nationalised guthi land) or the Department of Archaeology."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/schema"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P16_used_specific_object",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "used specific object"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Quantification: many to many (0,n:0,n)\n\nScope note:\nThis property describes the use of material or immaterial things in a way essential to the performance or the outcome of an instance of E7 Activity. \nThis property typically applies to tools, instruments, moulds, raw materials and items embedded in a product. It implies that the presence of the object in question was a necessary condition for the action. For example, the activity of writing this text required the use of a computer. An immaterial thing can be used if at least one of its carriers is present. For example, the software tools on a computer.\nAnother example is the use of a particular name by a particular group of people over some span to identify a thing, such as a settlement. In this case, the physical carriers of this name are at least the people understanding its use.\n\nExamples:\n- The writing of the scope note of the CIDOC CRM property “P16 used specific object” contained in the CIDOC CRM version 4.1 (E7) used specific object Nicholas Crofts’ computer (E22) mode of use Typing Tool; Storage Medium (E55). [the original scope note was later extended in the CIDOC CRM version 4.3] \n- The people of Iraq calling the place identified by TGN ‘7017998’ (E7) used specific object “Quyunjig” (E41) mode of use current; vernacular (E55).\n\nProperties:\n- P16.1 mode of use: E55 Type\n\nIn First Order Logic:\n- P16(x,y) ⇒ E7(x)\n- P16(x,y) ⇒ E70(y)\n- P16(x,y) ⇒ P12(x,y)\n- P16(x,y) ⇒ P15(x,y)\n- P16(x,y,z) ⇒ [P16(x,y) ∧ E55(z)]"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
