  {
    "@id": "http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P100i_died_in",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "died in"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Quantification of the inverse property: one to many, necessary (1,n:0,1)\n\nScope note of the inverse property:\nThis property links an instance of E69 Death to the instance of E21 Person that died.\nAn instance of E69 Death may involve multiple people, for example in the case of a battle or disaster.\nThis is not intended for use with general natural history material, only people.\n\nExamples of the inverse property:\n- Mozart’s death (E69) was death of Mozart (E21). (Sitwell, 2017)\n\nIn First Order Logic of the inverse property:\n- P100(x,y) ⇒ E69(x)\n- P100(x,y) ⇒ E21(y)\n- P100(x,y) ⇒ P93(x,y)"
      }
    ]
  },
