  {
    "@id": "http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P34_concerned",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "concerned"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#inverseOf": [
      {
        "@id": "http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P34i_was_assessed_by"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Quantification: many to many, necessary (1,n:0,n)\n\nScope note:\nThis property identifies the instance of E18 Physical Thing that was assessed during an instance of E14 Condition Assessment. \nConditions may be assessed either by direct observation or using recorded evidence. In the latter case the instance of E18 Physical Thing does not need to be present or extant at the time of assessment.\n\nExamples:\n- The condition assessment of the silver collection in 1997 (E14) concerned silver cup 232 (E22). (fictitious)\n- The condition assessment of the cover of MS Sinai Greek 418 (E14) concerned the cover of MS Sinai Greek 418 (E22). (Honey and Pickwoad, 2010)\n- The condition assessment of the endband cores of MS Sinai Greek 418 (E14) concerned the endband cores of MS Sinai Greek 418 (E22). (Honey and Pickwoad, 2010)\n\nIn First Order Logic:\n- P34(x,y) ⇒ E14(x)\n- P34(x,y) ⇒ E18(y) \n- P34(x,y) ⇒ P140(x,y)"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E55_Type",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Type"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Scope note:\nThis class comprises concepts denoted by terms from thesauri and controlled vocabularies used to characterize and classify instances of CIDOC CRM classes. Instances of E55 Type represent concepts, in contrast to instances of E41 Appellation which are used to name instances of CIDOC CRM classes. \nE55 Type provides an interface to domain specific ontologies and thesauri. These can be represented in the CIDOC CRM as subclasses of E55 Type, forming hierarchies of terms, i.e. instances of E55 Type linked via P127 has broader term (has narrower term): E55 Type. Such hierarchies may be extended with additional properties.\n\nExamples:\n- weight, length, depth [types for instances of E54 Dimension]\n- portrait, sketch, animation [types for instances of E36 Visual Item]\n- French, English, German [types for instances of E56 Language]\n- excellent, good, poor [types for instances of E3 Condition State]\n- Ford Model T, chop stick [types for instances of E22 Human-Made Object]\n- cave, doline, scratch [types for instances of E26 Physical Feature]\n- poem, short story [types for instances of E33 Linguistic Object]\n- wedding, earthquake, skirmish [types for instances of E5 Event]\n\nIn First Order Logic:\n- E55(x) ⇒ E28(x)"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/FearlessnessAssessment",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/ontology"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Fearlessness Assessment"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/RitualAssessment"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The 'fearlessness test' popularly described (animal heads, masked dancers in darkness). Its historical and contemporary practice is DISPUTED -- some former Kumaris contest how it is described. Represented as an assessment whose accounts are source-attributed via hg:hasAssertion, NOT as a mandatory criterion."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/schema"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Deliberately not a SelectionCriterion subclass: popular accounts and lived testimony disagree. Attach each account as a Assertion (CRMinf I2 Belief) with its own source and confidence."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P29_custody_received_by",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "custody received by"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Quantification: many to many (0,n:0,n)\n\nScope note:\nThis property identifies the instance(s) of E39 Actor who receive custody of an instance of E18 Physical Thing in an instance of E10 Transfer of Custody. \nThe property will typically describe Actors receiving custody of an object when it is handed over from another Actor’s care. On occasion, physical custody may be received involuntarily or illegally, e.g. through accident, unsolicited donation, or theft.\nIn reality, custody is either transferred to someone or from someone, or both.\n\nExamples:\n- Representatives of The National Gallery (E74) received custody through the delivery of the paintings by Secure Deliveries Inc. to the National Gallery (E10).\n\nIn First Order Logic:\n- P29(x,y) ⇒ E10(x)\n- P29(x,y) ⇒ E39(y) \n- P29(x,y) ⇒ P14(x,y)"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/GuthiType#SanaGuthi",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual",
      "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept",
      "https://cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/GuthiType"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/ontology"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Sana Guthi"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Funeral association (sanah guthi): the association that organises the funeral procession and cremation of member households; in some localities it and the si guthi are one body."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/scheme/GuthiType"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Sana Guthi"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/dataQualityNote",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain": [
      {
        "@id": "http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crminf/I2_Belief"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/ontology"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Data quality note"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range": [
      {
        "@id": "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#PlainLiteral"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Notes on data quality, completeness, or uncertainty."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/schema"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "http://purl.org/pav/version",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#AnnotationProperty"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E6_Destruction",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Destruction"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Scope note:\nThis class comprises events that destroy one or more instances of E18 Physical Thing, such that they lose their identity as the subjects of documentation.\nSome destruction events are intentional, while others are independent of human activity. Intentional destruction can be documented by classifying the event as both an instance of E6 Destruction and of E7 Activity. \nThe decision to document an object as destroyed, transformed, or modified is context-sensitive: \n1. If the matter remaining from the destruction is not documented, the event is modelled solely as an instance of E6 Destruction. \n2. An event should also be documented using E81 Transformation if it results in the destruction of one or more objects and the simultaneous production of others using parts or material from the original. In this case, the new items have separate identities. Matter is preserved, but identity is not.\n3. When the initial identity of the changed instance of E18 Physical Thing is preserved, the event should be documented as an instance of E11 Modification.\n\nExamples:\n- the destruction of Herculaneum by volcanic eruption in 79 AD (Camardo, 2013)\n- the destruction of Nineveh (E6, E7) (River, 2016) \n- the breaking of a champagne glass yesterday by my dog (fictitious)\n\nIn First Order Logic:\n- E6(x) ⇒ E64(x)"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasInfluencedBy",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov-o#"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@value": "wasInfluencedBy"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Because prov:wasInfluencedBy is a broad relation, its more specific subproperties (e.g. prov:wasInformedBy, prov:actedOnBehalfOf, prov:wasEndedBy, etc.) should be used when applicable."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:n908fb06c9e6143c3be2ae96a746557e3b514",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#AllDifferent"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#distinctMembers": [
      {
        "@list": [
          {
            "@id": "https://cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/RitualType#Abhisheka"
