  {
    "@id": "http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E3_Condition_State",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Condition State"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Scope note:\nThis class comprises the states of objects characterised by a certain condition over a time-span. \nAn instance of this class describes the prevailing physical condition of any material object or feature during a specific instance of E52 Time-Span. In general, the time-span for which a certain condition can be asserted may be shorter than the real time-span, for which this condition held.\nThe nature of that condition can be described using P2 has type. For example, the instance of E3 Condition State “condition of the SS Great Britain between 22(nd) September 1846 and 27(th) August 1847” can be characterized as an instance “wrecked” of E55 Type.\n\nExamples:\n- the \"reconstructed\" state of the “Amber Room” in Tsarskoje Selo from summer 2003 until now (Owen, 2009)\n- the \"ruined\" state of Peterhof Palace near Saint Petersburg from 1944 to 1946 (Maddox, 2015)\n- the state of my turkey in the oven at 14:30 on 25(th) December 2002 [P2 has type: E55 Type “still not cooked”] (fictitious)\n- the topography of the leaves of Sinai Printed Book 3234.2361 on the 10(th) July 2007 [described as: of type \"cockled\"] (fictitious)\n\nIn First Order Logic:\n- E3(x) ⇒ E2(x)"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/scheme/DatePrecision",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#ConceptScheme"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Date Precision (concept scheme)"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Controlled vocabulary for the temporal precision of recorded heritage dates."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Date Precision (concept scheme)"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/Container",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/ontology"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "obsolete Container"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#deprecated": [
      {
        "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean",
        "@value": true
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Root container for cultural heritage data instances. PLACEMENT RULE: put each instance in the most specific collection available (e.g. a Festival in festivals, not ritual_events; a Person in persons, not actors); broader collections exist for instances of the general classes only."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Deprecated in HeritageGraph 1.1.0: a LinkML serialization construct for JSON/YAML data files, not a domain term. It is defined in HeritageGraph-container.yaml and used only by the JSON Schema; RDF instance data should not use it."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/schema"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P107i_is_current_or_former_member_of",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "is current or former member of"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Quantification of the inverse property: many to many (0,n:0,n)\n\nScope note of the inverse property:\nThis property associates an instance of E74 Group with an instance of E39 Actor that is or has been a member thereof.\nInstances of E74 Group and E21 Person may all be members of instances of E74 Group. An instance of E74 Group may be founded initially without any member. \nThis property is a shortcut of the more fully developed path from E74 Group, P144i gained member by, E85 Joining, P143 joined to E39 Actor.\nThe property P107.1 kind of member can be used to specify the type of membership or the role the member has in the group.\n\nExamples of the inverse property:\n- László Moholy-Nagy (E21) is current or former member of Bauhaus (E74). (Moholy-Nagy, 2012)\n- National Museum of Science and Industry (E74) has current or former member The National Railway Museum (E74). (Rolt, 1971)\n- The married couple Queen Elisabeth and Prince Phillip (E74) has current or former member Prince Phillip (E21) kind of member husband (E55). (Brandreth, 2004)\n\nIn First Order Logic of the inverse property:\n- P107(x,y) ⇒ E74(x)\n- P107(x,y) ⇒ E39(y)\n- P107(x,y,z) ⇒ [P107(x,y) ∧ E55(z)]\n- P107(x,y) ⇐ (∃z) [E85(z) ˄ P144i(x,z) ˄ P143(z,y)]"
      }
    ]
  },
