  {
    "@id": "http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P9i_forms_part_of",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "forms part of"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Quantification of the inverse property: many to many (0,n:0,n)\n\nScope note of the inverse property:\nThis property associates an instance of E4 Period with another instance of E4 Period that is defined by a subset of the phenomena that define the former. Therefore, the spacetime volume of the latter must fall within the spacetime volume of the former.\nThis property is transitive and asymmetric.\n\nExamples of the inverse property:\n- Cretan Bronze Age (E4) consists of Middle Minoan (E4). (Hood, 1971)\n\nIn First Order Logic of the inverse property:\n- P9(x,y) ⇒ E4(x)\n- P9(x,y) ⇒ E4(y)\n- P9(x,y) ⇒ P10(y,x)\n- [P9(x,y) ∧ P9(y,z)] ⇒ P9(x,z)\n- P9(x,y) ⇒ ¬P9(y,x)"
      }
    ]
  },
