    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Quantification: many to many (0,n:0,n)\n\nScope note:\nThis property describes the inclusion relationship between two instances of E52 Time-Span.\nThis property supports the notion that the temporal extent of an instance of E52 Time-Span falls within the temporal extent of another instance of E52 Time-Span. It addresses temporal containment only, and no contextual link between the two instances of E52 Time-Span is implied. This property is transitive and reflexive.\n\nExamples:\n- The time-span of the Apollo 11 moon mission (E52) falls within the time-span of the reign of Queen Elizabeth II (E52). (Riley, 2009; Robinson, 2000)\n\nIn First Order Logic:\n- P86(x,y) ⇒ E52(x) \n- P86(x,y) ⇒ E52(y)\n- [P86(x,y) ∧ P86(y,z)] ⇒ P86(x,z)\n- P86(x,x)"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/yearOffsetFromGregorian",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/domainIncludes": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/CalendarSystem"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/ontology"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Year offset from gregorian"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range": [
      {
        "@id": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Number of years this calendar runs ahead of the Gregorian (Common Era) year at the start of its year (e.g. 57 for Bikram Sambat, -879 for Nepal Sambat). Because the calendar's new year does not fall on 1 January (see hg:epochDateGregorian), a year-precision date Y converts to the two-year interval [Y - offset, Y - offset + 1] CE; the single year Y - offset is correct only for dates between the calendar's new year and 31 December. A calendar whose new year is 1 January converts to Y - offset exactly."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/schema"
      }
    ]
  },
